#use(std/code)

#section{Media and Block Elements}::

#code{#image}, #code{#blockquote} and #code{#hr} from #code{std/base}
#code{#image(url alt: "…")} is inline and #strong{requires} the #code{alt:}
text; #code{#blockquote} and #code{#hr} are block-level (they break the
surrounding paragraph)

#code(lang: "plain")
	Above the rule.

	#blockquote{A short quotation.}

	#blockquote
		A longer quotation spanning
		its own indented block.

	#hr

	Here is #image("cat.png" alt: "a napping cat") shown inline.

	#pagebreak

	Content after a page break.

Live:

Above the rule

#blockquote{A short quotation.}

#blockquote
	A longer quotation spanning
	its own indented block

#hr

Here is #image("cat.png" alt: "a napping cat") shown inline

#pagebreak

Content after a page break

Corner cases:

#list
	#item
		#code{#pagebreak} is a block-level page break: HTML #tt{<div style="break-after: page">}, Typst #code{#pagebreak()},
		and a raw HTML block in Markdown (which has no native page break)
	#item
		Omitting #code{alt:} is an error, on purpose — a missing alt is invisible to sighted authors while breaking screen
		readers. For a purely decorative image pass an explicit #code{alt: ""}
	#item
		The function is #code{#blockquote}, not #code{#quote} — #code{quote} is a Racket special form, and a call named
		#code{quote} would never reach the library
	#item: #code{#hr} is a zero-argument void element: no args, no body

#section{Images}
	#code{#image(src alt: "…" width: w height: h loading: "lazy")} — only #code{alt:} is required. A #strong{local} src
	is a file path relative to the document that names it (like #code{#include}); its pixel size is read from the file
	header (PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP) and emitted as #code{width} / #code{height}, so the browser reserves the space before
	the image loads. The cat above renders as:

	#code(lang: "plain")
		<img src="cat.png" width="96" height="64" alt="a napping cat" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

	#list
		#item
			#code{width:} / #code{height:} are positive integers (CSS pixels) and override the file; given only one, the
			other follows the image's aspect ratio
		#item
			#code{loading:} is #code{"lazy"} (default) or #code{"eager"} — use #code{"eager"} for an above-the-fold hero
			image. #code{decoding="async"} is always emitted
		#item
			A src with a scheme (#code{https:}, #code{data:}, …) or a protocol-relative #code{//host/…} is remote and
			passes through untouched: no file access, no size unless you give one
		#item
			A root-relative #code{/static/x.png} resolves under the CLI's #code{--root} directory; without
			#code{--root} it is left untouched (the site root is not knowable)
		#item
			#code{?query} and #code{#fragment} are ignored and #code{%XX} escapes decoded for the file lookup; the
			emitted src is always the author's spelling
		#item
			A missing local file, or one that is not a PNG/JPEG/GIF/WebP, is a located error. SVG has no intrinsic
			pixel size: it must exist, but only explicit dimensions are emitted
		#item
			JPEG EXIF orientations 5–8 (rotated 90°) report transposed dimensions, matching how browsers lay them out
		#item
			Responsive layouts need #code{img { max-width: 100%; height: auto }} in the stylesheet — otherwise a
			shrunken image keeps its attribute height and stretches
		#item
			Markdown and Typst output ignore the size and loading attributes (#code{![alt](src)} /
			#code{#image("src", alt: …)})
		#item
			The emitted src is not rewritten for the output location: an image named from an #code{#include}d file in
			another directory, or a single-mode build that inlines such a page, keeps its source-relative spelling

	#section{Asset Resolver Hook}
		The #code{src} and size attributes come from the #code{current-image-resolver} parameter (#code{yamd/image}): a
		procedure #code{(src path width height) -> attrs}. #code{path} is the resolved local file, or #code{#f} for a
		remote / unresolvable src; #code{width} / #code{height} are the explicit arguments or #code{#f}; the result is
		an association list of attributes holding at least #code{src}. #code{#image} adds #code{alt}, #code{loading}
		and #code{decoding} unless the resolver already set them. #code{default-image-resolver} does the sizing above;
		wrap it to rewrite src, e.g. a cache-busting plugin on #code{PLTCOLLECTS}:

		#code(lang: "racket")
			#lang racket/base
			(require file/sha1 yamd/image)
			(provide cache-bust)

			(define (cache-bust)
			  (define next (current-image-resolver))
			  (current-image-resolver
			   (lambda (src path width height)
			     (define attrs (next src path width height))
			     (if path
			         (cons (cons 'src (format "~a?h=~a" src (call-with-input-file path sha1)))
			               (remove (assq 'src attrs) attrs))
			         attrs))))

		A document opts in with #code{#use(site/assets)} then #code{#cache-bust}; every later #code{#image} of that
		document (and the files it embeds, #code{#toctree} children included) goes through it. The parameter is
		rebound per document evaluation, so the setting never leaks into the parent or the next document. An
		#code{#image} built inside a delayed (second-pass) node runs after that evaluation, with the outer resolver and
		source directory. Install it from a call, not the plugin's module body: a plugin is
		instantiated once per process. A Racket embedder can instead #code{parameterize} it around #code{build-docset}
